// Copy is explanatory, not a claim that the simplified model computes legal tax liability.
export const HELP={
 gross:['Gross household income','All household earnings before income taxes, retirement contributions, insurance premiums, and other paycheck deductions. Add the annual gross income of the adults whose expenses are included. A $5,000 gross monthly paycheck is $60,000 a year. Use consistent household totals throughout.'],
 takeHome:['Take-home pay','What is left after the modeled income tax, payroll tax, and pre-tax retirement contribution. Here health premiums are listed separately under Healthcare. If using a paycheck to calibrate your tax rates, do not subtract those premiums a second time.'],
 age:['Household reference age','Choose the age of one adult who represents this household, and use that same person consistently. This drives the historical age-group context and the ages at which future contributions stop or withdrawals begin. It does not change mortgage eligibility.'],
 down:['Down payment','The portion of the purchase price you pay upfront, excluding closing costs. On a $400,000 home, 20% down is $80,000 and the starting loan is $320,000. A 0% entry is a mathematical scenario, not a promise that a zero-down loan is available.','cfpb'],
 closing:['Closing costs','Fees and other costs due when the home purchase is completed, in addition to the down payment. CFPB describes a typical range of 2–5% of the home price. The editable 3% here is a planning assumption; use your Loan Estimate when available. Credits and exact prepaid items are not modeled.','cfpb'],
 reserve:['Cash to keep untouched','Cash you want left after the down payment and closing costs, for emergencies, repairs, moving, or other priorities. This is a balance kept aside, not an additional monthly expense. Include that cash within “Total cash you’re starting with” and specify here how much must remain.'],
 buffer:['Monthly breathing room','An extra amount you want left over after housing, living expenses, taxes, debt payments, and the savings contributions already entered. This reduces the home budget. It is not a second emergency-fund contribution; it is an unassigned margin for surprises.'],
 mortgage:['Fixed-rate mortgage','A loan secured by your home. This calculator models equal monthly principal-and-interest payments for the selected term and rate. Taxes, insurance, HOA, maintenance, and utilities are added separately. It does not model adjustable rates, points, loan qualification, or FHA/VA/USDA-specific charges.'],
 interest:['Mortgage interest rate','The annual interest rate charged on the remaining loan balance, not the APR including fees. A fixed rate stays constant over this model’s term. Use a lender quote when available; the starting number is an example, not a live market rate.'],
 principal:['Principal & interest','Principal repays what you borrowed. Interest pays for borrowing. Your monthly principal-and-interest payment stays level on the modeled fixed-rate loan, while the share going toward principal rises over time. Other ownership costs are separate.'],
 propertyTax:['Property tax','A recurring tax imposed on property. Here it is estimated as a percentage of the purchase price each year, divided by 12. Actual assessments, exemptions, special districts, and future reassessments differ by location. Enter a local estimate; this is not a jurisdiction-specific tax calculation.'],
 insurance:['Homeowners insurance','Coverage for specified property damage and liability. Enter the monthly premium; divide an annual quote by 12. Flood, earthquake, or wind coverage may require separate policies. Add those premiums to this amount if needed.'],
 hoa:['HOA dues','Homeowners association fees charged by some neighborhoods and condominiums. Enter monthly dues, or convert annual dues to a monthly amount. Special assessments are extra and should be planned for in your reserve or other spending.'],
 maintenance:['Maintenance reserve','Money set aside for repairs and replacement, such as a roof, heating system, appliances, or plumbing. Here it is a percentage of home value each year, divided by 12. The starting 1% is only a scenario assumption; the age and condition of the home can change the amount substantially.'],
 pmi:['Private mortgage insurance (PMI)','Insurance that protects the lender if a borrower defaults. It is commonly required on conventional mortgages with less than 20% down. This model applies your chosen annual PMI rate to the starting loan amount, divided by 12, only below 20% down. It does not predict when PMI will end, and other loan programs have different rules.','pmi'],
 incomeTax:['Effective income-tax rate','An estimate of federal, state, and local income taxes combined, excluding payroll taxes. Effective means an average share, not your highest marginal bracket. The model applies this rate to gross income after the entered pre-tax 401(k) contribution. It does not calculate tax brackets, credits, filing-status rules, or other deductions.'],
 payroll:['Effective payroll-tax rate','An estimated share of gross pay for Social Security and Medicare. The example 7.65% is the common employee rate below the Social Security wage cap; actual effective rates vary with earnings, number of earners, additional Medicare tax, and self-employment. Pre-tax 401(k) contributions generally do not reduce these taxes. Enter an effective household estimate, not an exact tax schedule.','irs'],
 k401:['401(k), 403(b) & traditional retirement accounts','Employer retirement plans let you invest for retirement. Traditional pre-tax employee contributions generally reduce current taxable income, but remain subject to Social Security and Medicare. In the household budget, enter employee contributions per month; employer matching does not reduce your take-home pay. In net worth, enter the account balance. Forecast additions may include vested employer contributions. Eligibility, annual limits, vesting, and withdrawal taxes are not calculated.','irs'],
 roth:['Roth IRA & Roth accounts','A Roth IRA is an individual retirement account funded with after-tax money. Contributions are not deductible; qualified withdrawals can be tax-free when the applicable rules are met. A Roth 401(k) is a separate employer-plan arrangement. Budget fields ask for monthly contributions; net-worth fields ask for current balances. This calculator does not determine eligibility or contribution limits.','roth'],
 brokerage:['Taxable brokerage','An investment account holding assets such as stocks, bonds, or funds outside a retirement account. Use its current market value, not just what you deposited. Returns are uncertain; dividends, gains, fees, and taxes can affect results. Forecast returns here should be your assumption after ongoing fees and any annual tax drag.'],
 emergency:['Emergency fund','Cash set aside for unexpected expenses or an interruption in income. The home-budget field is a monthly contribution. In net worth, this is the total amount already saved. If you list an emergency fund separately, exclude it from your checking and other savings balances to avoid double-counting.'],
 college:['529 education savings','A tax-advantaged account used for eligible education expenses. Rules govern permitted expenses, tax treatment, and withdrawals. Include only the value your household owns, once. Planned contributions are monthly in the household budget; the net-worth field is today’s balance.'],
 hsa:['Health savings account (HSA)','A tax-advantaged account available to eligible people with qualifying health coverage. Funds can pay qualified medical expenses and may be invested. Enter the current balance you own. This worksheet does not check eligibility, contribution limits, or withdrawal tax treatment.'],
 netWorth:['Net worth','What you own minus what you owe. Add current asset values, then subtract remaining debt balances. A $300,000 home with a $220,000 mortgage contributes $80,000 to net worth. Enter the full home value in assets and the mortgage in debts; do not also add the home equity. Income and future paychecks are not assets.'],
 resale:['Current resale value','An estimate of what you could sell an item for today, not its original purchase price or the cost of buying a replacement. Include significant items such as computers, furniture, jewelry, cameras, instruments, or tools. Selling costs and taxes are not deducted automatically.'],
 depreciation:['Vehicle depreciation','The percentage of remaining value lost each year. The editable 10% default is a midlife-car scenario informed by Kelley Blue Book’s 8–12% annual range after the first two years; it is not universal. New vehicles often lose 20% or more in year one. Enter a rate appropriate to age, model, mileage, and condition. At 10%, a $20,000 vehicle becomes $18,000 after one year and $16,200 after two.','kbb'],
 belongings:['Belongings depreciation','A scenario for how much resale value an item loses each year. The editable 20% is an illustrative starting assumption, not a measured average. Electronics, furniture, tools, and collectibles behave differently. Use 0% for a constant-value scenario. Enter appreciating collectibles under “Business & other assets” with an appropriate growth assumption.'],
 return:['Annual growth / total return','Your assumed effective annual change in value, including reinvested earnings. This is an input, not a promised return. A 6% annual assumption turns $1,000 into $1,060 after one year without additions. The model uses an equivalent monthly rate so 12 months compound to exactly the chosen annual return. Negative rates can model losses.'],
 compound:['Compound growth','Growth can earn more growth. With no additions, $10,000 growing 6% annually becomes $10,600 after one year and $11,236 after two. Contributions here arrive at the end of each month. Markets do not deliver steady returns; a constant-rate projection is a scenario, not a forecast of market prices.'],
 contributions:['Monthly additions','New money added to a cash or investment account at each month’s end until the selected stopping age. Use money you can fund from outside these listed balances; transfers between listed accounts should not be counted as new savings. Enter personal additions here; employer matching has its own field and must not be counted twice. Use Budget savings to link the matching accounts, or keep independent manual additions. Optional annual contribution growth applies until the stopping age.'],
 inflation:['Inflation & today’s dollars','Inflation reduces buying power. “Today’s dollars” divides a future amount by (1 + your inflation assumption) raised to the number of years. This makes future purchasing power easier to compare with current money. The default 2.5% is a scenario assumption, not a forecast.'],
 debt:['Remaining debt balance','The amount still owed today, not the original loan or its monthly payment. Include mortgages, auto and student loans, credit cards, personal loans, unpaid taxes, and other liabilities. Enter each debt once. For projections, the contractual interest rate and monthly payment determine the remaining balance.'],
 apr:['Debt annual interest rate','Use the contractual interest rate charged on your balance. For a mortgage or installment loan, use the note rate rather than an APR that includes fees. For credit cards, use the applicable purchase or balance APR as a simplified rate estimate. The model charges annual rate ÷ 12 each month, then applies the payment. Daily interest, changing rates and fees may differ. Mortgage payments here exclude escrow for tax and insurance.'],
 withdrawal:['Monthly portfolio withdrawals','Money removed from cash, then investments, starting at the selected withdrawal age. The amount is fixed in nominal dollars unless Increase withdrawals with inflation is enabled. That option treats the entered amount as today’s dollars and increases it from today, including years before withdrawals start. HSA and 529 balances count toward net worth but are excluded from general withdrawals unless explicitly included. Property and belongings are not automatically sold. Any amount that cannot be funded is shown as a shortfall. Taxes, required distributions, pensions, and Social Security are not modeled.'],
 dti:['Debt-to-income ratio (DTI)','Monthly modeled mortgage principal, interest, property taxes, homeowners insurance, HOA, and PMI, plus car loan and other debt payments, divided by gross monthly income. It excludes living expenses and maintenance. Lenders use additional rules; this informational ratio does not determine approval.'],
 stress:['Stress test','Try a less comfortable scenario without overwriting your base inputs. Mortgage and property-tax changes are percentage points: 6% plus 1 point becomes 7%. Lifestyle and healthcare changes multiply their costs: $100 plus 15% becomes $115. Auto insurance and utilities receive the lifestyle adjustment. Turn it off to restore the baseline.'],
 benchmark:['Historical age-group context','The Federal Reserve’s 2022 Survey of Consumer Finances reports family medians grouped by the reference person’s age. Figures shown are 2022 dollars; income refers to the preceding calendar year. These are historical comparisons, not today’s targets or a personal financial grade. Household definitions and assets counted here may differ.','fed'],
 scenario:['Saved scenarios','A named snapshot of every calculator input, forecast setting, and selected tab. It is stored in this browser on this device, not in a shared database. Other visitors cannot see it. Clearing site data removes it. Use a private device if your figures are sensitive.']
};
